Playwright + Cucumber Java Framework Guide

Project: PlaywrightJava
Location: c:\Users\arunn\eclipse-workspace\PlaywrightJava

1. Overview
This document explains the framework built in this project: how tests are organized, which Playwright APIs and locator types were used, how Cucumber integrates (features/steps/runner), what was automated in Maven to prepare Playwright, and specifics of each method used in the sample step implementations. It also lists troubleshooting tips and how to run tests locally.

2. Project structure (relevant paths)
- pom.xml — Maven build file with dependencies and exec plugin to prepare Playwright binaries
- src/test/resources/features/*.feature — Cucumber feature files (sample.feature, dummy-browser.feature)
- src/test/java/steps/*.java — Cucumber step definitions that exercise Playwright
  - SampleSteps.java — example using Playwright to open example.com (with Jsoup fallback)
  - DummyBrowserSteps.java — uses the dummy-browser.html to exercise a variety of locators
- src/test/java/runner/RunCucumberJUnit4Test.java — JUnit4 test runner annotated with @RunWith(Cucumber.class)
- src/test/resources/pages/dummy-browser.html — local practice page with inputs, table, list, modal, hidden banner
- src/test/resources/pages/example.html — local fallback example page
- src/test/resources/cucumber.properties — cucumber configuration (features, glue, plugins)

3. How tests run (high level)
- mvn test triggers Maven lifecycle: generate-test-resources -> test-compile -> test.
- exec-maven-plugin is configured in generate-test-resources phase to ensure Node package exists, install @playwright/test and run npx playwright install --with-deps (this downloads browser binaries).
- JUnit4 picks up the runner class RunCucumberJUnit4Test which uses Cucumber to run .feature scenarios.
- Steps use Playwright Java API to create a browser, open pages, and interact. After each scenario, resources are cleaned up in @After hooks.

4. Cucumber integration
- Features: Written in Gherkin and placed under src/test/resources/features. Example: dummy-browser.feature.
- Glue: step definitions are in package steps. cucumber.properties sets cucumber.glue=steps and cucumber.features=classpath:features.
- Runner: RunCucumberJUnit4Test.java is a small JUnit4 runner with @RunWith(Cucumber.class) and @CucumberOptions to point to features, glue and reporting plugins.

5. Dummy page (practice harness)
- src/test/resources/pages/dummy-browser.html includes: text input (username), password, textarea (message), checkbox (newsletter), radio buttons (role), select (country), buttons (submit/open modal), table (users), list (menu), hidden banner and modal.
- Each element has attributes useful for testing: id, data-testid, labels and text content.

6. Locators used (detailed) — with examples from the code
Playwright supports many locator strategies. The step implementations demonstrate the following:

- getByLabel("LabelText")
  Use when an input is associated with a <label>. Example:
    page.getByLabel("Username").fill("playwright-user");
  This finds the form control matching the visible label text.

- getByTestId("testid")
  When your HTML includes data-testid attributes for stable test targeting:
    page.getByTestId("username-input").inputValue();

- getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON/AriaRole.RADIO, new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Name"))
  Semantic queries by ARIA role and accessible name. Example clicking a button:
    page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Submit")).click();
  Great for accessibility-friendly selectors.

- CSS selectors via locator("css")
  Generic CSS targeting (IDs, attributes, nested structures):
    page.locator("#password").fill("secret");
    page.locator("[data-testid='country-select']").selectOption("uk");
    page.locator("table tbody tr").count();

- locator().nth(index)
  Pick a specific element from a collection:
    page.locator("li").nth(2).textContent();

- allTextContents() and textContent()
  Get visible text of a list/element:
    page.locator("[data-testid='menu-list'] li").allTextContents().contains("Profile");

- isVisible() and isHidden()
  Check visibility state of UI (used for modal and banner checks):
    page.getByTestId("modal-dialog").isVisible();
    page.getByTestId("modal-dialog").isHidden();

- check() for checkboxes/radios and selectOption() for selects
    page.getByTestId("newsletter").check();
    page.locator("[data-testid='country-select']").selectOption("uk");

7. Playwright concepts explained
- Playwright vs browser engines: Playwright is a Java API that drives browser engine processes (Chromium, Firefox, WebKit) via a bundled Node helper. The Java client starts a Node-based driver behind the scenes to control browsers.

- Playwright object: entry point in Java. Created by Playwright.create(). Holds references to browser types.
  - Playwright playwright = Playwright.create();
  - BrowserType chromium = playwright.chromium();

- BrowserType and Browser: launch() on a BrowserType (chromium, firefox, webkit) starts a browser process.
  - Browser browser = playwright.chromium().launch(new BrowserType.LaunchOptions().setHeadless(true));

- BrowserContext (not explicitly created in these samples): an isolated browser profile. Use browser.newContext() to create separate contexts for parallel tests (cookies/storage isolation).

- Page: like a tab. Created with browser.newPage() or context.newPage(). You call page.navigate / page.goto / page.click etc. to interact.

- Locator: Playwright’s main API for interacting with elements. Methods are lazy and auto-waiting: they wait for elements to be actionable.
  - page.locator(selector) returns a Locator, which supports click(), fill(), isVisible(), etc.

- Auto-waiting: Playwright retries actions until the element is ready or a timeout occurs — reduces flakiness compared to raw sleeps.

- Selectors: Playwright supports CSS, text selectors, role selectors, test id selectors and custom selector engines.

- Tracing, recording, HAR, and debugging: Playwright can record traces and network HARs to analyze test runs.

- Headless vs headful: headless runs without UI, headful shows the browser window. For debugging, set headless=false.

- Playwright Java uses a node helper under the hood. Therefore, the Node environment (npx, package.json, and installed playwright-core) and downloaded browser binaries are required for full functionality.

8. Playwright methods used in this project (what they do)
- Playwright.create()
  - Starts Playwright Java runtime. Initializes resources needed to launch browsers.

- playwright.chromium()/playwright.firefox()/playwright.webkit()
  - Access to browser engine types.

- BrowserType.launch(LaunchOptions)
  - Starts a browser process. Common options:
    .setHeadless(true|false)
    .setSlowMo(milliseconds) — slow down actions for debugging

- browser.newPage()
  - Opens a new page/tab.

- page.navigate(url) or page.goto(url)
  - Navigates page to the URL. Use file:// path for local HTML files.

- page.getByLabel(string)
  - Query an input by its associated label text.

- page.getByTestId(string)
  - Query element with data-testid attribute.

- page.getByRole(AriaRole, options)
  - Query element by ARIA role (button, radio, checkbox) and accessible name.

- page.locator(selector)
  - Create a Locator using a CSS (or selector engine) string. Supports chaining and collection operations (nth, first, last).

- locator.fill(value)
  - Set value of an input/textarea.

- locator.inputValue()
  - Read the current value of an input element.

- locator.check()/locator.uncheck()
  - Check or uncheck checkboxes/radios.

- locator.selectOption(value)
  - Select option in a select element. Can pass value or label.

- locator.click()
  - Click an element. Locator auto-waits until clickable.

- locator.count()
  - Return number of matching elements.

- locator.allTextContents()
  - Get list of visible text for matching elements.

- locator.isVisible()/isHidden()
  - Check visibility state of element.

- locator.textContent()/locator.innerText()
  - Get textual content — innerText differs in whitespace handling.

- page.content()
  - Get the full HTML content of the current page (useful for fallback parsing with Jsoup).

- page.title()
  - Get document title.

- browser.close()/page.close()/playwright.close()
  - Close resources in reverse order.

9. Playwright + Maven automation notes
- The project adds an exec-maven-plugin block to ensure Node/npm environment has @playwright/test and to run npx playwright install --with-deps to download browser binaries.
- On CI or developer machines, it’s often better to run npm install and npx playwright install manually once (or cache the ms-playwright folder) to avoid repeated downloads.
- On Windows, the Playwright installer reports missing native system DLLs if host OS is missing optional libraries. Those are host-specific and must be resolved by installing the appropriate runtime dependencies.

10. Fallbacks and resilience added
- SampleSteps.java: added a Jsoup fallback path: if Playwright cannot start or page content retrieval fails, the step loads a local static HTML page (src/test/resources/pages/example.html) and performs the same checks with Jsoup to allow the automated test run to continue in constrained environments.
- DummyBrowserSteps uses only Playwright but will throw useful assertion errors if a condition fails.

11. How to run the tests locally (recommended)
Pre-requisites:
- Java 11+ and Maven installed
- Node.js and npm installed (required by Playwright Java to prepare node helper and browsers)

Steps:
1. From project root, install Node packages (if not using Maven automation):
   npm init -y
   npm i -D @playwright/test
   npx playwright install --with-deps
   (This ensures the node helper and browser binaries are available.)

2. Run tests:
   mvn test
   or run a single runner:
   mvn -Dtest=RunCucumberJUnit4Test test

Notes:
- If you see Node errors referencing missing package.json or MODULE_NOT_FOUND for package.json, run the npm init/install steps above.
- The exec-maven-plugin tries to automate npm init and npm install, but running installation manually gives clearer control.

12. Troubleshooting
- Error: Cannot find module './../../package.json'
  - Solution: ensure package.json exists and @playwright/test (or playwright-core) is installed under project node_modules. Running npm install @playwright/test resolves this.

- Playwright Host validation missing libraries (icutu77.dll, psl-5.dll)
  - These are OS-level dependencies (Windows). Install the required redistributables or required GTK/system libs for Linux containers. On Windows, ensuring the system has up-to-date Visual C++ redistributables and supporting libs may help.

- Tests hang or fail due to missing browsers
  - Run npx playwright install and verify ms-playwright folder under user profile has browser executables.

13. Summary of methods & locators used (quick reference)
- getByLabel("text") — label-based input selector
- getByTestId("id") — data-testid attribute
- getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON/ARIA..., new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Name"))
- locator("css") — CSS selectors
- locator(...).nth(i), .count(), .allTextContents()
- fill(), inputValue(), selectOption(), check(), click(), isVisible(), isHidden(), textContent(), innerText(), content(), title()
- Playwright.create(), playwright.chromium().launch(), browser.newPage(), page.navigate(), page.close(), browser.close(), playwright.close()

